data: {"done":true,"conversationId":"cmvookwu20061tptuhmlsunsv","durationMs":2463,"partial":false,"messageId":"cmvookyqi0065tptutem14jph","userMessageId":"cmvookwu40063tptud1pk48gk","content":"The sky appears blue because of a process called Rayleigh scattering. When sunlight reaches Earth's atmosphere, it interacts with gas molecules and tiny particles. These molecules scatter the sunlight in all directions, but they scatter shorter wavelengths (blue and violet light) much more effectively than longer wavelengths (red and yellow light).\n\nSince our eyes are more sensitive to blue light than violet, and some of the violet light gets absorbed higher in the atmosphere, the sky predominantly looks blue to us during the day. This is also why sunsets appear red or orange - when sunlight has to travel through more atmosphere, most of the blue light gets scattered away, leaving the warmer colors visible.","meta":{"states":["UNVERIFIED"],"sources":[],"verification":[],"stages":[{"stage":"verifying","durationMs":0}],"degraded":["search not warranted: no freshness signals; answerable from stable knowledge; semantic router: This is a stable scientific explanation about light and atmosphere."],"searchMode":"auto"}}

